---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the minimum IELTS score to get into a Canadian university?",
    answer:
      "The standard minimum IELTS Academic overall band score is 6.5 at UBC, the University of Toronto, McGill, Waterloo, SFU, and York. Most of these universities also require a minimum of 6.0 in each individual band (Reading, Listening, Writing, Speaking). Waterloo is stricter: it requires 6.5 in both Writing and Speaking even though the overall minimum is also 6.5. Meeting the minimum makes you eligible for review -- it does not guarantee admission. Competitive applicants at selective programmes typically score 7.0 or above.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is CELPIP accepted at Canadian universities for undergraduate admission?",
    answer:
      "No. CELPIP is not listed as an accepted English proficiency test at any major Canadian university for undergraduate admission. CELPIP is an immigration test accepted by IRCC for Express Entry and permanent residency applications. If your child is applying to a Canadian university, they need IELTS Academic, TOEFL iBT, PTE Academic, or Duolingo English Test -- not CELPIP.",
  },
  {
    question: "Has the TOEFL scoring scale changed recently?",
    answer:
      "Yes. On January 21, 2026, ETS changed the TOEFL iBT total score scale from 0-120 to 0-6. Section scores also changed. University admissions pages are being updated to reflect the new scale. TOEFL scores on both scales are being accepted during the transition period. Always check the university's current admissions page to confirm which scale their stated minimum refers to.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does my child need an English test if they have studied in English for several years?",
    answer:
      "Many universities offer an exemption if a student has completed at least three or four consecutive years of full-time study at an English-medium institution in an approved country. UBC, U of T, McGill, Waterloo, and York require four years; SFU requires only three years -- the shortest of the six universities listed here. If your child arrived in Canada in Grade 10 or earlier and completed secondary school here, they may qualify for an exemption without taking any test. Confirm the exact policy with each university for the current admission cycle.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do all programmes at a university use the same English test minimum score?",
    answer:
      "No. Most Canadian universities set a general minimum and then allow individual faculties or programmes to require higher scores. Nursing, education, social work, and some business and engineering programmes commonly require higher scores -- sometimes 7.0 overall IELTS Academic with no individual band below 7.0 or 6.5. Always check the requirements for the specific programme, not just the university-wide page.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    {
      name: "English Score Requirements at Top Canadian Universities: The 2026 Reference Table",
      path: "/blog/english-score-requirements-canadian-universities/",
    },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="English Score Requirements at Top Canadian Universities: The 2026 Reference Table"
  description="Verified IELTS Academic, TOEFL, PTE Academic, and Duolingo minimum scores for UBC, U of T, McGill, Waterloo, SFU, and York. From official admissions pages. Updated May 2026."
  type="article"
  keywords="English test requirements Canadian universities, IELTS score UBC, IELTS score University of Toronto, TOEFL Canadian university, PTE Academic Canadian university"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag ic-eyebrow">Reference</span>
      <h1 class="ic-display" style="margin: 0;">English score requirements at top Canadian universities: the 2026 reference table</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>May 29, 2026</span>
        <span>Dr. Kara Abdolmaleki</span>
        <span>12 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Every number in this article was pulled directly from official university admissions pages
        in May 2026. Sources are linked at the foot of the main table so you can verify them before
        any application deadline.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things to understand before reading the table. First, these are <strong>minimum
        eligibility scores</strong>, not competitive targets. Meeting the minimum means your
        child's application will not be declined on language grounds alone. It does not mean
        admission is likely at a selective programme. For most students applying to the universities
        below, a safe working target is <strong>IELTS Academic 6.5 to 7.0 overall</strong>, with
        individual bands no lower than 6.0 and ideally 6.5. Preparing only to hit the minimum in
        a competitive cycle is a meaningful risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, one test that appears repeatedly in conversations about English and Canada is
        completely absent from every university's requirements page: <strong>CELPIP is not
        accepted at any of these universities</strong>. CELPIP is a Canadian immigration test.
        If someone has told your child that CELPIP satisfies a university language requirement,
        that is incorrect. See
        <a href="/blog/why-celpip-pte-core-wont-get-into-canadian-university/">Why CELPIP and PTE Core will not get your child into a Canadian university</a>
        for a full explanation. For a broader look at choosing between the accepted tests, the
        <a href="/blog/ielts-toefl-pte-duolingo-canadian-university-comparison/">IELTS Academic vs TOEFL vs PTE Academic vs Duolingo comparison</a>
        covers format, cost, and preparation in detail.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card">
      <h2 class="ic-display">TOEFL scale change: what you need to know</h2>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          On <strong>January 21, 2026</strong>, ETS changed the TOEFL iBT total score scale from
          0-120 to a new 0-6 scale. Per-section scores also changed. University admissions pages
          are being updated in stages, and some institutions still list only the old-scale minimum
          during the transition.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the table below, TOEFL scores are shown as <strong>old scale (0-120) / new scale
          (0-6)</strong> wherever both are confirmed. Where only one scale has been published by
          the university, that is the figure shown. If your child is submitting a TOEFL score, check
          the university's current admissions page to confirm which scale their requirement
          references.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">2026 minimum score comparison: six universities</h2>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          Scores shown are overall minimums. Per-band or per-skill requirements are indicated in
          each cell and explained in detail in the notes section below the table. Exemption years
          indicate the minimum number of consecutive years of full-time English-medium instruction
          required to waive the test. Check each university's current admissions page before relying
          on any figure here.
        </p>
      </div>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>University</th>
          <th>IELTS Academic</th>
          <th>TOEFL iBT</th>
          <th>PTE Academic</th>
          <th>Duolingo</th>
          <th>CAEL</th>
          <th>Exemption</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>UBC</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall; min. 6.0 each band</td>
          <td>90 (old) / 4.5 (new)</td>
          <td>65 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>125</td>
          <td>70</td>
          <td>4 years</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>University of Toronto</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall; min. 6.0 each band</td>
          <td>89 (old) / 4.5 (new)</td>
          <td>65 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>120</td>
          <td>70 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>4 years</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>McGill</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall; min. 6.0 each band</td>
          <td>90 (old) / 4.5 (new)</td>
          <td>65 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>125</td>
          <td>70 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>4 years</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Waterloo</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall; Writing 6.5, Speaking 6.5, Reading 6.0, Listening 6.0</td>
          <td>90 (old) / 4.5 (new); Writing 25 (old) / 5.0 (new), Speaking 25 (old) / 5.0 (new)</td>
          <td>63 overall; Writing min. 65, Speaking min. 65</td>
          <td>120 overall; Literacy 125, Production 125</td>
          <td>Verify at uwaterloo.ca</td>
          <td>4 years</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>SFU</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall; min. 6.0 each band</td>
          <td>88 (old) / 4.5 (new)</td>
          <td>65 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>125</td>
          <td>70 overall; min. 60 each section</td>
          <td>3 years</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>York</strong></td>
          <td>6.5 overall</td>
          <td>88 (old) / 4.5 (new)</td>
          <td>60</td>
          <td>120</td>
          <td>60</td>
          <td>4 years</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top: var(--s-2); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--c-text-muted, #666);">
        Sources (verified May 29, 2026):
        <a href="https://you.ubc.ca/applying-ubc/requirements/english-language-competency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UBC</a> &middot;
        <a href="https://future.utoronto.ca/english-language-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U of T</a> &middot;
        <a href="https://www.mcgill.ca/undergraduate-admissions/apply/english-proficiency" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McGill</a> &middot;
        <a href="https://uwaterloo.ca/future-students/admissions/english-language-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Waterloo</a> &middot;
        <a href="https://www.sfu.ca/students/admission/admission-requirements/english-language-requirement.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SFU</a> &middot;
        <a href="https://futurestudents.yorku.ca/requirements/language-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">York</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Five things about the table that are easy to miss</h2>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">1. Waterloo requires 6.5 in Writing and Speaking, not just overall</h3>
      <p>
        Every other university on this list sets a single overall IELTS minimum with a lower
        per-band floor (typically 6.0). Waterloo is different. Its 6.5 Writing and 6.5 Speaking
        minimums apply even though the overall minimum is also 6.5. This means a student with an
        overall score of 6.5 who receives 6.0 in Writing does not meet Waterloo's requirement,
        full stop. A student with 7.0 overall but 6.0 in Speaking also falls short. The consequence
        is that students targeting Waterloo must treat Writing and Speaking as hard targets at 6.5,
        not as skills where a lower score can be offset by Reading or Listening.
      </p>
      <p>
        This elevated standard carries through to other tests. For PTE Academic, Waterloo requires
        Writing and Speaking each at 65, even though the overall minimum is the lower figure of 63.
        For TOEFL iBT, Writing and Speaking sections must reach 25 (old scale) or 5.0 (new scale).
        For the Duolingo English Test, Literacy and Production subscores must each reach 125 despite
        the overall minimum being 120.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">2. SFU's exemption is three years, not four</h3>
      <p>
        Simon Fraser University grants the English-medium instruction exemption after three
        consecutive years of full-time study, compared to four years at UBC, U of T, McGill,
        Waterloo, and York. This distinction is practically significant for students who arrived
        in Canada in Grade 10. Three completed years of Canadian secondary schooling may be
        sufficient for the SFU exemption. The same student applying simultaneously to UBC would
        still need a test score.
      </p>
      <p>
        For families applying to both SFU and at least one other university on this list, it is
        worth checking exemption eligibility at each institution independently. A student can be
        exempt at SFU and still required to test at the others. Do not assume a single exemption
        determination applies everywhere.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">3. York's PTE Academic and CAEL minimums are lower than the other universities</h3>
      <p>
        York University's published PTE Academic minimum is 60. The corresponding figure at UBC,
        U of T, McGill, and SFU is 65. York's CAEL minimum is also 60, against 70 at UBC, McGill,
        and SFU. This is not an error. York has set lower language thresholds for these two
        particular tests. For IELTS and TOEFL, York's requirements are close to the group average.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters for families using PTE Academic or CAEL specifically. A student who achieves
        a PTE Academic score of 62 does not meet the requirement at UBC, U of T, McGill, or SFU,
        but does meet the general requirement at York. Note that individual York faculties may have
        their own higher requirements.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">4. McGill requires scores to be sent directly from the test provider</h3>
      <p>
        McGill does not accept self-reported test scores, nor does it accept PDF copies uploaded by
        the applicant. Scores must be transmitted directly from the testing organisation to McGill.
        For IELTS, this means requesting an official institution report to be sent to McGill's
        registered institutional receiving code via the British Council or IDP candidate account.
        For TOEFL, scores are sent from the ETS My TOEFL account using McGill's Designated
        Institution code. Allow adequate lead time between the test date and the application
        deadline. Score reporting is not instantaneous, and delays have held up applications.
        McGill publishes its institutional receiving codes on its admissions requirements page.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">5. Most universities have elevated thresholds for specific programmes</h3>
      <p>
        The figures in the table are university-wide general minimums. They are the floor below
        which applications are not reviewed for language. Individual faculties and programmes
        regularly require higher scores. Health science programmes, nursing, education, social work,
        and some business programmes at multiple universities have set higher thresholds --
        sometimes IELTS Academic 7.0 overall with no individual band below 7.0 or 6.5. Always
        locate the requirements page for the specific programme your child is applying to, not
        only the general university admissions page. Assuming the general minimum applies to every
        programme is a mistake that is difficult to correct after an application has been submitted.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">University-by-university detail</h2>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">UBC (University of British Columbia)</h3>
      <p>
        UBC publishes a clear, stable set of English proficiency requirements. The IELTS Academic
        minimum of 6.5 overall with 6.0 per band is well established. UBC accepts Duolingo at 125,
        and PTE Academic at 65 overall with 60 per section, making it one of the more flexible
        institutions for test choice among the six. The CAEL minimum at UBC is 70. The four-year
        English-medium exemption policy is clear, and UBC explicitly notes that French immersion
        education in Canada does not qualify as English-medium instruction for exemption purposes.
        UBC is one of Canada's most selective institutions; meeting the language minimum is a
        prerequisite, but it is far from sufficient for competitive programme admission.
        <a href="https://you.ubc.ca/applying-ubc/requirements/english-language-competency/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official UBC English language competency requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">University of Toronto</h3>
      <p>
        U of T's requirements are closely aligned with UBC and McGill with two small differences:
        the TOEFL minimum is 89 on the old scale (not 90), and Duolingo is accepted at 120 (not
        125). The CAEL acceptance at 70 overall with 60 per section is notable -- U of T is one of
        the universities where CAEL is a genuine option. U of T's per-programme variation is
        significant, particularly for Applied Science and Engineering, where language expectations
        in the upper tier of accepted applicants are consistently higher than the stated minimum.
        <a href="https://future.utoronto.ca/english-language-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official U of T English language requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">McGill University</h3>
      <p>
        McGill's score requirements sit at the upper tier of the group: IELTS 6.5 with 6.0 per
        band, TOEFL 90 (old) / 4.5 (new), PTE Academic 65 with 60 per section, Duolingo 125, and
        CAEL 70 with 60 per section. The four-year exemption is standard. The institution-specific
        policy to be aware of is the requirement for direct score submission from the test provider.
        McGill is also a bilingual institution but does not grant automatic English exemptions to
        students from Francophone Quebec backgrounds -- the standard English-medium instruction
        criteria apply.
        <a href="https://www.mcgill.ca/undergraduate-admissions/apply/english-proficiency" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official McGill English proficiency requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">University of Waterloo</h3>
      <p>
        Waterloo's per-skill requirements for Writing and Speaking set it apart from the rest of
        this group. Students who are confident readers and listeners but less assured in productive
        skills should note this asymmetry carefully and build preparation specifically around
        Writing and Speaking performance. Waterloo does not list CAEL as an accepted test, which
        makes it the narrower of the six universities in terms of accepted options. The TOEFL
        new-scale (0-6) equivalents for some section minimums had not been separately published as
        of May 2026; if your child is taking TOEFL under the new scale, confirm with Waterloo's
        admissions office for the section minimums that apply.
        <a href="https://uwaterloo.ca/future-students/admissions/english-language-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official Waterloo English language requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">Simon Fraser University (SFU)</h3>
      <p>
        SFU's score requirements are comparable to UBC and McGill across all accepted tests. The
        TOEFL minimum (88 old scale) is slightly below UBC and McGill (90), and the CAEL, PTE
        Academic, and Duolingo minimums align with the upper tier. The three-year English-medium
        exemption is the genuine differentiator. For students in British Columbia specifically,
        the combination of comparable requirements and the shorter exemption threshold makes SFU
        worth evaluating carefully when planning a test strategy alongside UBC.
        <a href="https://www.sfu.ca/students/admission/admission-requirements/english-language-requirement.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official SFU English language requirements</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">York University</h3>
      <p>
        York's IELTS Academic minimum of 6.5 overall is consistent with the group, but York does
        not publish per-skill IELTS minimums for general undergraduate programmes in the same
        explicit way the other institutions do. The TOEFL minimum is 88 (old scale) / 4.5 (new
        scale). The notably lower PTE Academic minimum (60) and CAEL minimum (60) distinguish York
        from the rest of the group for students taking those tests. Duolingo is accepted at 120.
        York's Schulich School of Business and Glendon campus (bilingual) maintain their own
        requirements.
        <a href="https://futurestudents.yorku.ca/requirements/language-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official York language test requirements</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">What "meeting the minimum" actually means for admission</h2>
      <p>
        Meeting a university's English proficiency minimum is an eligibility condition, not an
        admissions advantage. It clears one disqualifier from the application. It does not move
        the application forward in the queue.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mechanics are straightforward. Applications that fall below the minimum score are
        declined at the language screening stage, typically before a human reads the rest of the
        file. Applications that meet or exceed the minimum enter the general review pool, where they
        compete against every other applicant on a full range of criteria: grades, prerequisite
        subjects, personal statements, activity records, programme fit, and available places.
      </p>
      <p>
        In competitive programmes at UBC, U of T, and McGill, many of the students who are
        actually admitted hold IELTS Academic scores of 7.0 or higher. This is not because 7.0
        is required -- it is because the pool of admitted students typically brings both strong
        academic profiles and strong language scores. A student presenting 6.5 IELTS is in
        the eligible range. Whether they are in the competitive range depends on everything else
        in the application.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also a practical risk-management argument for targeting above the minimum. Test
        performance on any given day can be affected by illness, nerves, or examination conditions.
        A student targeting exactly 6.5 who has a difficult day may come in at 6.0 in one band
        and find their application ineligible. A student who has been preparing toward 7.0 and has
        a difficult day is more likely to still clear the minimum. The buffer matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        The recommendation is not to spend months over-preparing for a language test at the expense
        of grades, subject preparation, or the personal statement. It is to treat 6.5 as the
        floor and 7.0 as the working target -- a meaningful but achievable goal for a student
        with adequate preparation time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Why IELTS Academic is the safe default for Canadian university admission</h2>
      <p>
        When a family has no specific reason to prefer one test over another, IELTS Academic is
        the most defensible starting choice. There are five reasons for this.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Universal acceptance.</strong> IELTS Academic is accepted by every major Canadian
        university without exception. No other test matches this coverage. TOEFL iBT comes close,
        but gaps in acceptance exist for some smaller institutions and certain programme types.
        Using IELTS Academic means your child will never arrive at an admissions page and find the
        test unaccepted.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Institutional familiarity.</strong> Canadian university admissions staff have
        evaluated IELTS Academic scores for decades. The band descriptors are well understood in
        the Canadian academic context. When a file shows 6.5 IELTS Academic, reviewers know
        precisely what that represents. This is not yet uniformly true of newer tests like the
        Duolingo English Test, whose score meaning is still being calibrated in institutional
        memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Preparation ecosystem.</strong> The volume of available preparation resources for
        IELTS Academic exceeds that of any other English proficiency test. Official practice
        materials, past papers, reputable preparation books, online courses, and specialist
        tutors are all more readily available for IELTS Academic than for the alternatives. This
        is a practical advantage during the preparation period.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Score portability.</strong> IELTS Academic scores are accepted beyond university
        admission. Many scholarship applications, some regulated professional licensing bodies in
        Canada (including nursing, engineering, and other fields), and immigration pathways all
        reference IELTS Academic specifically. If your child's path may include professional
        licensing or further immigration steps, a strong IELTS Academic score has uses beyond
        the undergraduate application.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Live speaking assessment.</strong> The IELTS Academic speaking component is a
        live, face-to-face interview with a trained examiner. Some students perform better in a
        conversational setting with a real person than they do speaking into a microphone for
        an automated scoring system. If your child is more at ease in live conversation, the IELTS
        speaking format may produce a stronger result than the computer-based speaking tasks on
        TOEFL or PTE Academic.
      </p>
      <p>
        For students who have a specific reason to prefer another test -- a dual application to
        both Canadian and American universities pointing toward TOEFL, a tight deadline favouring
        PTE Academic's 48-hour results, or a clear cost constraint pointing toward Duolingo -- the
        alternatives are legitimate. But as a default with no strong reason to deviate, IELTS
        Academic is the most reliable choice for a family planning a Canadian university
        application.
      </p>
      <p>
        For guidance on which version of IELTS to take, see
        <a href="/blog/ielts-academic-vs-general-training-university/">IELTS Academic vs General Training: which version does your teen need?</a>
        The distinction matters and the wrong version cannot be substituted after the fact.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 class="ic-display">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {pageFaqs.map((faq) => (
        <details class="guide-faq-item">
          <summary class="guide-faq-question">{faq.question}</summary>
          <p class="guide-faq-answer">{faq.answer}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card guide-cta" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Prepare your child to score above the minimum</h2>
      <p>
        Our tutors work specifically with secondary students preparing IELTS Academic for
        Canadian university applications. The goal is not just to clear 6.5 -- it is to build
        toward 7.0 and above, putting your child in the range typical of competitive applicants
        at the universities on this list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not sure yet whether your child needs a test at all? The five-minute quiz checks for
        exemption eligibility based on schooling history.
      </p>
      <div class="cluster" style="gap: var(--s-3); margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        <a href="/tutoring/" class="btn btn-primary ic-btn">IELTS Academic tutoring</a>
        <a href="/ielts/does-my-child-need-english-test-quiz/" class="btn btn-ghost">Does my child need a test? Take the quiz</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
